@node Overview
@chapter Overview
@cindex Overview

The io package contains functions for reading and writing various type of data files
and some additional file utilities.  The main data file categories are:

@itemize
@item
.csv and other delimited text files.  The funcions csv2cell and cell2csv in the io
package can process mixed numeric / text data.  Octave's dlmread/dlmwrite and csvread
/csvwrite can only process numeric data.

@item
Spreadsheet files.  Out of the box, functions are provided for reading / writing data
and exploring contents from/to .xlsx (MS-Excel), .ods (LibreOffice Calc) and
.gnm / .gnumeric (Gnumeric) spreadsheet files.  For other file formats and sometimes
faster I/O, external software can be invoked through 'interfaces'.  Note that the
spreadsheet I/O functions are meant for transferring data; formatting etc. is ignored.

@item JSON  Although Octave provides jsonencode and jsondecode functions, the io
package contains the (contributed) functions fromJSON and toJSON that support e.g.,
complex numeric data and some other constructs.  Support for fromJSON and toJSON is
limited.

@item Various other, less used formats, often for dedicated software.  Most of the
related functions were kindly provided by external contributors.  Support for these
functions is also limited, if only because the io package maintainers have no access
to the pertinent SW.
@end itemize

@c -------------------------------------------------------------------------
@node Installing and loading
@chapter Installing and loading
@cindex Installing and loading

The io package must be installed and then loaded to be used.

It can be installed in @acronym{GNU} Octave directly from octave-forge,

@section Windows install
@cindex Windows install
If running in Windows, the package may already be installed, to check run:

@example
pkg list io
@end example

@section Installing
@cindex Installing
With an internet connection available, the io package can be installed from
packages.octave.org using the following command within @acronym{GNU} Octave:

@example
pkg install io
@end example

The latest released version of the package will be downloaded and installed.

Otherwise, if the package file has already been downloaded it can be installed
using the following command in @acronym{GNU} Octave:

@example
pkg install io-@value{VERSION}.tar.gz
@end example

@section Loading
@cindex Loading
Regardless of the method of installing the package, in order to use its functions,
the package must be loaded using the pkg load command:

@example
pkg load io
@end example

The package must be loaded on each @acronym{GNU} Octave session.

