@node Spreadsheet I/O Usage
@chapter Spreadsheet I/O Usage
@cindex Spreadsheet I/O Usage
For spreadsheet I/O use is made of "interfaces"; an interface is just terminology
for a connection to software installed on the computer.  An exception is the (default)
@code{'OCT'} interface which comprises just Octave language and functions and as such needs
no external SW.  @xref{XREFextsw, ,Invoking external spreadsheet I/O SW}, below for more
details.  For an extensive overview of supported interfaces, see the help for the
@code{xlsopen} function.

@section Efficient Spreadsheet I/O
@cindex Efficient Spreadsheet I/O
The functions @code{xlsread} and @code{xlswrite} are provided in the io package.  Note that
for every individual read/write operation these functions must read the entire spreadsheet
file from disk and, in case of writing, write it back as well.  Therefore including these
functions in a for loop will be unduly time-consuming, and so they are mostly useful for
occasionally reading/writing data from a spreadsheet. @*
When repetitively reading data from or writing to optionally multiple different sheets and/or
different cell ranges in one spreadsheet file, the io package provides functions for
(1) opening a spreadsheet file, (2) extracting and adding data, and (3) closing the
spreadsheet and possibly writing a changed spreadsheet back to disk.  These functions are:

@itemize
@item @code{xlsopen} - opens a spreadsheet file and returns a pointer that contains a
connection to the spreadsheet file contents.

@item @code{xls2oct} - using the pointer, extracts raw data from a specific sheet and/or a
specific cell range. @*
The function @code{parsecell} can be used to separate numeric and text data into separate
double and cellstr arrays, resp.

@item @code{oct2xls} - using the pointer, adds data to a specific cell range and/or specific
sheet.

@item @code{xlsclose} - closes the connection to the file contents and if these have changed,
writes it all back to disk.
@end itemize

Once a speadsheet file has been 'opened' and a pointer is returned, operations for extracting
and adding can be mixed at will.  When used this way the spreadsheet @emph{file} is read just
once (and written just once) for possibly multiple data extraction and adding operations.  In
fact, @code{xlsread} and @code{xlswrite} in the io package are mere wrappers around these
functions. @*
It is even possible to have multiple spreadsheet files open simultaneously, e.g., for
transferring data, optionally after processing, from one spreadsheet to another.

@strong{Caution}: do not forget to invoke @code{xlsclose} after finishing spreadsheet I/O
operations to avoid stale pointers, data and maybe even external SW needlessly keeping RAM
and scratch disk space occupied. 

@section Exploring spreadsheet files
@cindex Exploring spreadsheet files
The function @code{xlsfinfo} can be used for exploring the contents of spreadsheet files, i.e.,
the names and types of individual sheets in the file and occupied cell ranges in all sheets.

@section Invoking external spreadsheet I/O SW
@cindex Invoking external spreadsheet I/O SW
@anchor{XREFextsw}
Most external SW interfaces for spreadsheet I/O are Java based.  The required Java .jar
files (collections of Java methods) must be added to the javaclasspath.  On Windows, this 
will happen automatically if these files are put in a subdirectory 'Java\' in the user's
profile. @*
The windows package contains an interface to the Windows ActiveX system library, which
can be used to connect to various installed SW.  In the io package only an interface to
MS-Excel is provided ('@code{COM}'); a similar interface to LibreOffice Calc was contributed
to the Octave project but hasn't been integrated in the io package. @*
The io package provides the function @code{chk_spreadsheet_support} that automatically runs
various checks for required external SW for the interfaces supported by the io package and
optionally can find and connect the required SW (adding to the javaclasspath).  On Windows
systems, this function is run when the io package is loaded.  When unloading the io package,
this function will remove the connections to external SW that it set up when loading the io
package.  On *nix systems (including MacOS) this is disabled because the location of the
various required Java SW is not sufficiently standardized.

@section Extra options, Compatibility and Gotchas
@cindex Extra options, Compatibility and Gotchas
Below some features of spreadsheet I/O with the io package functions are highlighted.
@itemize
@item Occupied cell ranges. @*
@code{xlsfinfo} in the io package will also return the ranges of occupied cells in each sheet
in the spreadsheet file.  For the COM interface that may be less reliable because Visual
Basic often includes empty but formatted cells as well in the tally.  For some spreadsheet
file types, @code{xlsfinfo} can also return the types of sheets (data, graphics, ...).

The numeric and string arrays (and the raw arrays) returned by xlsread (as 1st, 2nd and 3rd
output argument, resp.) will be stripped from empty outer rows and columns, even if an explicit
cell range to read from was specified.  However this behavior can be changed by a dedicated
field in an option structure that can be given as input argument to @code{xlsread}.
@code{xlsread} can return occupied cell ranges within the user specified cell range as the last
output argument.

@item Returning formulas rather than formula results. @*
@code{xlsread} and @code{xls2oct} allow returning formulas (i.e., their text) rather than
computed values in a cell.  That is controlled by a dedicated field in an option structure that
can be given as input argument to these functions.  See @code{xls2oct} for more details.

@item Dates. @*
Spreadsheet programs usually store dates (or date-times) as a specially formatted text string.
However, in .xls files (Excel 97 / BIFF8 and Excel 95 / BIFF5) dates are stored as as the number
of days passed since Dec. 31, 1899 ("epoch"); dates from before that epoch are stored as text
strings.  @code{xlsread} and @code{xls2oct} infer from the filename extension what to do to and
will return dates as number of days passed since AD.  To that end, the number 693960 will be
added to dates read from .xls spreadsheets, and subtracted from datenums before writing them to
.xls.  But this is something to be aware of when reading data from .xls files and writing them
to .ods or .xlsx, or vice versa.

@item Active sheet. @*
When reading from or writing to a spreadsheet file, @code{xlsread} (@code{xlsopen}) and
@code{xlswrite} (@code{xlsclose}) ignore the "active" sheet.  That implies that @code{xlsread}
(@code{xls2oct}) by default reads from the first (leftmost) sheet in the stack
rather than from the active sheet.  Similarly, @code{xlswrite} (@code{xlsclose}) write by
default to the first / leftmost sheet.  Specify the required sheet number or -better- sheet
name to overwrite that automatic sheet selection.

@item Cells with errors. @*
Spreadsheet cells containing errors (e.g., due to wrong formulas or references in the spreadsheet)
that spreadsheet programs show as e.g., @code{#Value} are usually returned as blank cells.  But
not all such errors can be catched.  Spreadsheets often contain cached values; when cells pointed
to by formulas or references contain invalid info, the cached values from previous evaluations by
the spreadsheet can still be present, and these may be returned by @code{xlsread} and
@code{xls2oct}.

@item Retaining formatting of cells in when writing to file. @*
When adding or changing data in a worksheet and writing to file, cell formatting will be retained
as much as possible.  The one exception is with the OCT interface; that will wipe all cell
formatting in changed worksheets.  This is a known limitation of the OCT interface.
@end itemize

